---
code: whylog-corrupt
when-fires: `dorc why --last` found a durable but it is truncated or otherwise unparseable (a partial write, a clobbered file). plan/whylog.rs parse (`inv-no-throw`: malformed bytes are DATA, never a panic).
why: inv-no-throw: a corrupt durable is diagnostics, never a crash — the reader refuses politely and names the parse-failure reason. Pull-surface: Warning, Floor::None. The reason is typed: four WhylogCorruptReason variants, each with its own prose-component, which the `{detail}` hole renders (28L rul-reason-enums-not-sibling-codes). Remediation register (help) wanted: the durable is damaged — re-run the live analysis to regenerate it.
edit-loop: edit a sentence in the transcript below, then run `mise run loom:compile -- whylog-corrupt && mise run loom:promote -- whylog-corrupt`. `mise run loom:vars -- --all whylog-corrupt` lists this case's values; type {{name}} to insert or move one.
---
-- .whylog --
dorc-whylog/1 nonce=dorc attempt=0 host=fixture @@dorc@@

-- replay --
$ dorc why --last --whylog=.whylog
whylog: warning[whylog-corrupt]: the saved why-log is damaged and cannot be read
back (no dorc-whylog-end sentinel (a truncated write?))
   = help:  this usually means an interrupted write; re-run `dorc why` live, and
            the next plan or apply will save a fresh why-log
